WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.833
(mid-tempo music)

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.670
- Well, welcome back to another
episode of The Addy Hour."

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.140
It's always my honor and privilege

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.750
to host these conversations.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:14.560
And today, I'm grateful to be able to host

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.160
Jeremy Lin and Esther Choo

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.800
for conversation about
mental health, race,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.440
wellness, and culture
in a AAPI communities.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.560
So these are two individuals

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.100
that likely need no introduction
to you all as listeners,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:28.933
but I'm gonna go ahead

00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:30.820
and give them a brief
introduction, anyway.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.310
Starting out with Esther Choo.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.600
Dr. Choo is a physician

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.000
and popular health science communicator.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.748
She's known as bold and innovative voice

00:00:37.748 --> 00:00:40.140
on gender and racial equity in healthcare.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.680
And she's also advocate for new frameworks

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.840
for birthing positive and
productive workplaces.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.700
She graduated from Yale
University as undergrad,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.670
also went to the Yale School
of Medicine for med school,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.810
and currently has an NIH
funded research program

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.140
where she's looking at health
services and health policy

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.730
as a researcher.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.590
She's also the founding
member of Times Up Healthcare.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.680
She has also been very active
in the pandemic, as well,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.890
where she's helped raise awareness

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:06.940
about the critical shortage

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.435
of personal protective
equipment or PPE in hospitals.

00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:12.950
She also is a Chief
Medical Officer of Duke,

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.130
which builds mobile healthcare units.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.310
As someone who's been very active

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.090
in a lot of different roles.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.430
She also has numerous commentaries,

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.390
which regularly appear
in different places,

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.240
including the New England
Journal of Medicine.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.774
The Harvard Business Review,
NBC Thinks, (indistinct)

00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:28.800
and the Washington Post.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.900
She's also a regular
columnist for the Lancet

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.740
and she regularly
appears on CNN and MSNBC.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.173
So someone who is deeply invested

00:01:36.173 --> 00:01:38.740
in a lot of different spheres of influence

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.540
and who continues to make an
impact within her profession

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:42.970
and in the general community, as well.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.010
So I'm honored to welcome Dr. Esther too,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.220
to The Addy Hour podcast.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.449
Thanks for being here.

00:01:47.449 --> 00:01:49.640
- Thanks for having me on.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.843
- Of course.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.800
The second guest I'd like to introduce

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.890
is none other than Jeremy Lin.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.890
Jeremy is a Paulo Alto native,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.250
and of course is one of
the few Asian-Americans

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.150
to ever play in the NBA.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.470
Prior to his NBA career,

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.450
he actually played four seasons at Harvard

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.360
where he was an All Ivy first
team selection, two times,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.286
both in 2009 and 2010.

00:02:09.286 --> 00:02:10.590
As a Yale professor.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.860
I'm giving him a little bit of grace

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.450
for playing for the other team

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.420
partially because he
played for Coach Amaker

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.600
who was also a Duke
graduate, as I am myself.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.470
So I couldn't help myself, I
had to throw that in there,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.320
but Jeremy, as many of you all know

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.600
is someone who after being undrafted

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.160
and playing the NBA for a couple years,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.550
rose to a level of stardom

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.750
as Linsanity took off as
an overnight sensation.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.940
I actually remember the game

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.070
that he played against
the New Jersey Nets.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:40.430
I remember seeing that in real time

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.620
and just having known
who he was over the years

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.770
was really excited to see what
was happening in that moment.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.810
He, of course, has played for
lots of different NBA teams,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.360
including the Warriors,
the Knicks and the Rockets.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.330
He's currently playing
for the Beijing Ducks

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.270
in the 2021 to 2022 season

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:56.780
and I believe you all
are in the play offs,

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.471
so wanna wish him the best
of luck in that, as well.

00:02:59.471 --> 00:03:00.860
Also wanted to highlight

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.200
some of the different community things

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.550
that he's been involved in.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:08.440
In 2020, his Be The Light
campaign donated $1.4 million

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.030
to COVID relief efforts

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.950
and helped raise greater awareness

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.090
of increasing anti-Asian
racism during the pandemic.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:20.319
He's also been appointed
as UNICEF USA's ambassador

00:03:20.319 --> 00:03:22.390
and in that role he uses his platform

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.030
to champion children's rights

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.150
and to also help create more
equitable world for every child

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:32.565
His Jeremy Lin Foundation
also serves over 22,000 youth

00:03:32.565 --> 00:03:35.320
and they've done this through
grants, narrative change,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.840
community empowerment
and racial solidarity.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.270
So again, someone who
has used his platform

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.777
and continued to be really
invested in the community

00:03:42.777 --> 00:03:44.410
and I'm honored to welcome Jeremy Lin

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:46.810
to The Addy Hour podcast,
thanks for being here.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.981
- Thanks for having me. I'm excited.

00:03:50.981 --> 00:03:54.160
- Of course, and I'm glad to
have this come to fruition.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.520
This is something we've
actually been talking about

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.353
for quite a while

00:03:56.353 --> 00:03:57.980
and took some time to get
everything coordinated.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.260
So again, grateful to both of you

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.380
for being able to be here today.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.550
But as my listeners know, I
always like to just check in

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:06.620
and see how people are doing overall.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.840
So want to just get a sense
of how you're both doing,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:10.880
at this point in time

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.285
and also what your day to day looks like

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.220
if as much as you'd be willing to share.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.140
So Jeremy why don't we go in reverse order

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.090
and start with you.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.330
- Yeah, I currently, last night,

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.690
well, it's morning right now in China.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:28.770
And so last night,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.770
we got to the playoff
bubble in Ankang in China.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.980
And so the whole season has
been played in different bubbles

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.470
and these bubbles are in different cities.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.550
So we can only be in the
hotel and go to the gym

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.550
and the arena to compete
and then come back.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.540
And so we just got here,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.255
our first playoff game is in three days.

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:53.163
and from there it's gonna be,

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.930
just we'll see how far we go,

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.750
but it's gonna be four round playoffs

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.260
and that's basically it.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:06.260
Other than that, I've been over here

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:11.670
for the last about nine,
10 months for this season

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.900
and so it's about to wind down.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.130
We're about to see how
the season finishes,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.213
but everything else is great, I'm excited.

00:05:21.071 --> 00:05:23.880
And I'm excited to talk with Dr. Choo

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.643
and didn't realize we had a
lot of Yale background here.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.290
I wasn't warned of this when
I came into this podcast

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.580
but I think I'll do my best.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.498
No, we'll be okay.

00:05:38.498 --> 00:05:39.948
- You're gonna hold your own.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.660
- Let's just keep it civil
while we're recording,

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.653
and then it can all break down after that.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.700
- Well, definitely appreciate it.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.900
Maybe it's the reason we
didn't warn you ahead of time,

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.500
but like I said, I'm sure
you're gonna hold your own

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.330
and congrats on making the playoffs too.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.310
One quick follow up question.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:03.540
just as you were describing
the bubbles and all that,

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.470
what was it like coming
from here in the US?

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.200
Not that you haven't been in China before,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.330
but especially during this time

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.050
in transitioning into a different place

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.730
where there are different
protocols in place,

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:15.673
how has that process been for you?

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.860
- It's been the most
eye opening experience

00:06:23.476 --> 00:06:28.080
just to get to China,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.950
the amount of work, the amount of tests,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:32.820
things that you have to do,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.870
and I'll touch on this probably later in.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:39.630
Before I took off, I was negative.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:40.850
I landed, I was negative,

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.920
but on my third day of my
21 day hotel quarantine,

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.290
I tested positive for COVID.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:48.660
I still don't know how

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.420
and no one else on the
flight was positive.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:56.420
And I ended up spending 11
weeks in isolated quarantine

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:02.070
in a very small room in
the hospital, in the hotel,

00:07:04.243 --> 00:07:07.320
and so when I say like
the amount of, I mean,

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.410
we'll get into this later,

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:09.660
but it was almost three months

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.760
where I did not see the outside

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.360
or breathe any air from the outside

00:07:16.595 --> 00:07:19.167
and you couldn't do laundry,
you couldn't do anything.

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:21.770
It was quite an experience.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.210
And that definitely, we'll
talk about mental health

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.380
and all that later, but it is different.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:30.500
But then now here it's
a zero tolerance policy.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.420
And so when you're in a
country of 1.5 billion,

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.420
they basically have hardly
any cases, very, very few.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.640
and they're typically all
from people who are flying in.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.580
And so it's also very different

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.390
because when you're
walking around day to day,

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.640
there's essentially no threat.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.450
I mean, you are basically
living in a bubble already,

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.770
but we're in a bubble
inside of a bubble, yeah.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:56.964
- Wow, that's a totally different setup

00:07:56.964 --> 00:07:58.100
in a lot of ways.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.520
So, I mean, I appreciate you sharing that

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.660
and giving people some insight

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.697
into what that experience
has been like too.

00:08:02.697 --> 00:08:03.530
'Cause I think it's important for us

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.410
to realize how different things are moving

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.360
in different parts of the world.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.850
And then also, like you mentioned

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:09.440
some of the outcomes of that too,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.290
but I'm sure we'll loop back to that

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.840
just in terms of the mental
health component of that too,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.463
as you mentioned, so.
- Yeah.

00:08:16.982 --> 00:08:18.090
- Esther what about you?

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.670
How's life been for you these days

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:22.100
especially as an ER doctor

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.600
with everything that we're
continuing to navigate?

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:29.870
- Yeah, it's so interesting
to hear your story Jeremy,

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:32.900
because I mean, there's
that element of my life.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.360
that's about the ER,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:35.403
and it's kind of not what you think.

00:08:35.403 --> 00:08:38.480
I mean, we definitely have these surges

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.970
and in Oregon, the most recent surges,

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.430
the delta and omicron, were
really the most significant.

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:48.430
We dodged a lot of the
caseload from the early waves.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.220
It just didn't really hit Oregon hard

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.610
and then we were hit a harder later.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.700
But during the surges you
have a lot of empathy.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.330
Everyone's like, how are
you doing? Is the ER, okay?

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:02.240
But when you kind of mobilize
your energy and your spirit

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.073
for those surges

00:09:03.073 --> 00:09:05.260
and what I think, what
people don't realize

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:08.090
is that between the surges
is actually really hard too,

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.940
because that's when everything
comes crashing down on you.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:15.020
I mean, all of that care that was delayed,

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.100
people held off going to the hospital,

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.663
the surgeries were put off.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.780
We use a ton of people resources,

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.090
and then the surge is over

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.020
and all of that kind of
comes crashing down on you.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.310
And so, I mean, there's a piece

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:28.840
that just was published
by a friend of mine

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.280
in the Washington Post

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.490
that was enumerating all the shortages

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:33.840
that we have in the emergency department.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.580
I mean, supply chains are
still affected globally,

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:39.940
so we don't have things
like saline bags, IV tubing,

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.470
the ability to measure kidney function,

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.610
a host of medications that
you use every single day.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.440
Every other day is a
medication shortage notice.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:49.870
Like this drug that you need,

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.641
that you just think will always be there

00:09:51.641 --> 00:09:52.960
is in short supply.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.350
Periodically, different types of PPE,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.560
the protective gear still in short supply.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.070
And then the biggest one of course

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.610
is the human supply that
we're running out of

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:05.619
because we've lost a fifth
or more of our workforce,

00:10:05.619 --> 00:10:07.300
many, many nurses

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.290
we're always scrambling
just to fill shifts.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.790
And so I don't really remember

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.990
what normal was in the hospital before,

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:15.510
but we don't have it during surges,

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.150
we don't have it after surges.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.689
We're always diverting patients away

00:10:19.689 --> 00:10:21.172
as a specialty hospital.

00:10:21.172 --> 00:10:24.530
And so there is that whole element

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.975
and then I think just like what's striking

00:10:27.975 --> 00:10:29.210
as I listen to Jeremy,

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:33.640
tell a story about these like
incredibly rigorous processes

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:34.900
to keep people safe.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.300
And then I know you're
in a special NBA bubble

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.220
within that bubble,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.680
but I would say we are now in the US,

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.580
in this really surreal environment

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.330
where people are completely
not taking any precautions.

00:10:46.966 --> 00:10:49.120
There's still not adequate testing.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:50.943
We don't have surveillance
programs in most settings.

00:10:50.943 --> 00:10:52.570
I went to church the other day

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.300
and my family was the only one in masks,

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.910
with a third of the congregation elderly

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.483
and people coughing and
sneezing in a closed space.

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:06.010
And it's not even suggested to be careful

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:07.940
and just in a lot of places
in the United States,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.800
there's no concept of the fact

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.320
that there is a global pandemic happening

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:12.560
and we contribute to that

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.030
with our individual decision making.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.800
So that has been really
demoralizing to see for people

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.350
who are spending so much
time in the hospital

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.950
and sealing the tail end of that.

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.430
Because of course there's a lot of people

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:24.080
who because of misinformation

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:25.620
or because of their medical conditions

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.970
are not able to be vaccinated

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:32.220
to have good protections from coronavirus

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.270
when they're exposed.

00:11:34.275 --> 00:11:35.470
But that being said,

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:38.633
I mean, I've been in
the ER this entire time.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.850
I sometimes am not perfect about my PPE.

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.830
Somehow I feel like I should knock on wood

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:46.663
and I'm cursing myself,

00:11:46.663 --> 00:11:50.126
but somehow I have not gotten COVID yet.

00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:53.590
And so, you never take that for granted,

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:55.540
but we've been really blessed.

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:56.748
My husband is a physician also.

00:11:56.748 --> 00:12:01.300
We've both been exposed many times

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.310
in our community and at work

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:07.900
and so far our whole family
has stayed pretty healthy.

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.630
So just feeling very
lucky too, to this point

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.850
to have had that gift in our lives.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:15.653
- Yeah, definitely a
blessing in a lot of ways.

00:12:15.653 --> 00:12:18.180
I appreciate you just even
peeling back the layers

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.480
to just give all the perspectives.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.010
I think so much of the
time, it's easy for people

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.550
who aren't in the medical
setting day to day

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.170
to realize what's happening in between

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.220
and all the challenges
that are still there.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.280
So I appreciate that level of honesty

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:31.810
and just the realness of it too.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:32.880
But like you said also,

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.087
the blessing of not
having contracted COVID

00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:37.020
and just being able to
continue to walk through

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:39.670
in a lot of ways, so thanks for that.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.440
That perspective in a lot of ways,

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.033
to both of you, just for the PPCRA

00:12:43.033 --> 00:12:44.810
it's starting to bring out,

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:46.130
I know it's a lot we could unpack

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:49.330
from just those two
comments that you both made,

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:51.400
but one thing that we'll
do in the conversation too,

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:55.440
is also kind of lean
into AAPI space of things

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.770
to the Asian American
Pacific Islander component.

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:00.940
And I did just wanna lead
off just acknowledging

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.170
that that's a very imperfect
term in a lot of ways,

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:07.360
even though that's what we often
use in these conversations.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.193
So I wanted to make sure

00:13:08.193 --> 00:13:09.860
that that was also a point of emphasis

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:13.240
and also just with the
expectation that I would imagine

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.710
our listeners are not going
to be expecting the two of you

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:17.550
to speak on behalf of

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:22.187
the entire, racially
diverse AAPI communities

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:24.810
in that sense, as well,

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.240
but then also just having some insights

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.260
from your experiences too,

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.890
just based on the experiences
you've had in your own lives

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:32.910
and the interactions
with family and friends

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:33.743
and things like that.

00:13:33.743 --> 00:13:35.330
So not that those were disclaimers,

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:36.280
but I also just wanted to make sure

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.310
that we really, keep things in context

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:41.160
'cause it's so easy
sometimes to over generalize,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.010
but then it's also easy

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:43.650
to miss the important
components that are there.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.070
So as much as we can kind of
hold both of those together,

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:48.650
I think is important.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:49.483
But just to start out,

00:13:49.483 --> 00:13:51.180
I know we'll get into the
mental health component

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:52.296
a little bit too,

00:13:52.296 --> 00:13:53.129
but just wanted to hear a little bit

00:13:53.129 --> 00:13:56.370
about both of your experiences
in terms of growing up,

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.140
just in your communities en large

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:02.280
and how this aspect of being
a member of the AAPI community

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.730
has also factored into that.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.370
So Esther asked you if
we could start with you,

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.203
that'd be great.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:11.450
- Yeah, sure. I am the child of immigrants

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:14.450
and my parents moved here for grad school

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.630
from South Korea, what is now South Korea

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:19.000
and then never left.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.090
They kind of thought they were coming

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:20.973
just for my father's education.

00:14:20.973 --> 00:14:22.183
This is in the 1960s,

00:14:22.183 --> 00:14:26.206
so this is a very common story for Koreans

00:14:26.206 --> 00:14:27.780
who had a similar background,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.920
and ended up having their
kids here and then never left.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.913
So I was born here in the United States

00:14:31.913 --> 00:14:36.913
and we lived in a pretty, not
ethically diverse community

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:39.940
on the west side of Cleveland.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:42.570
And so I didn't grow up
around a lot of other Koreans,

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.320
but we did go to Korean
church for a while.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:45.780
And so that was kind of my exposure.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.720
So really almost the
entirety of my experience

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:53.020
with other Asians was my
family, my immediate family

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:54.130
and my extended family.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:55.680
But I think some of those things

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:59.700
really carried over nicely
to what I experienced

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.310
when I was part of a
larger Asian community

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:02.683
from college onwards.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.180
I think in terms of some of the topics

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:09.260
we're gonna talk about later on in this

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.430
or throughout this podcast,

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:15.430
I think, just, I don't
ever remember mental health

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.362
being part of the conversation.

00:15:17.362 --> 00:15:21.770
I do think kind of being
stoic and strong and resilient

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.070
and not sharing your feelings too much

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:26.160
was kind of the ethos in my family.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.320
We just weren't a warm,
it was very loving,

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.885
but there was not a
lot of warm, fuzzy talk

00:15:30.885 --> 00:15:33.630
and certainly we never spoke

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:35.180
about what I now understand

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:38.410
were a lot of mental health problems

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.070
across our extended family.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.480
You kind of understood
that somebody was ill

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:43.370
and had to go away for a while,

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.980
or there was just that
cousin who was very shy

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.821
and so never came out where
the rest of us were gathering.

00:15:48.821 --> 00:15:52.040
And only much later

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:53.430
when we started talking
about these things,

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:56.270
did we realize that we weren't perfect.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.740
What was happening is we just didn't talk

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:00.740
about things like
substance use, addiction,

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.707
mental health, suicide, those things

00:16:02.707 --> 00:16:07.707
and we're all given kinda euphemisms

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:11.480
or completely not addressed
at all and not talked about.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:14.885
So I think even mentioning
things like being depressed,

00:16:14.885 --> 00:16:17.920
or needing therapy or
needing to be on medications,

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:20.452
those are things I all
learned on my own as an adult

00:16:20.452 --> 00:16:22.920
and really had to reconcile them

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:27.374
against kind of the implicit
values and practices

00:16:27.374 --> 00:16:28.813
that I learned growing up.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:30.833
- I appreciate you sharing that

00:16:30.833 --> 00:16:33.890
and giving that perspective and
that honesty again, as well.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:35.240
Jeremy, what about for you?

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:42.120
- Yeah, my background
is pretty interesting.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.440
My grandparents born
and raised in Zhejiang,

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:53.275
which is a province

00:16:53.275 --> 00:16:55.913
and they grew up in a city,

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:59.830
like driving distance from Shanghai.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:03.810
Actually it was like 10 minutes
away from the COVID hospital

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:05.796
that I spent all that time at.

00:17:05.796 --> 00:17:10.796
And then my parents were
born and raised in Taipei

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:17.100
and then I was born and
raised in California.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.300
And so growing up was really interesting

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.350
because similar to Dr. Choo,

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:26.350
my main exposure to other Asians
was basically just at home

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:30.780
or when I would go to church.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:34.520
And I went to a Chinese church

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:38.270
and so there, it was like my Asian circle.

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:42.780
And I was also very fit
in with how I was raised

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:44.880
and the culture that I was raised in.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:46.570
But then I grew up in Paulo Alto,

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.810
which is primarily white community,

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.620
going to school, primarily white,

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:53.770
and Harvard, as well,

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:57.250
just having that kind
of school background.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.440
But then I would play basketball

00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.960
I'd play competitive basketball

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:01.666
I'm playing in the AU circuit.

00:18:01.666 --> 00:18:05.560
And so it felt like that was
primarily African American.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:08.380
And so it felt like I had
these three different worlds

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:09.213
that I lived in.

00:18:09.213 --> 00:18:11.690
And there was nobody that
could really understand,

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.850
if I had someone in one circle,

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:17.380
they might not understand what it was like

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:19.350
in one of the other circles.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.450
And there wasn't that much carryover

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:25.870
and so aside from being
at home and at church,

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:30.870
I was always kind of
like labeled a little bit

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:34.330
as like, because people
knew I played basketball,

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:36.570
but then, it was always
like, yo, he's the Asian,

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:37.840
he's Asian basketball player.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:38.730
That's the Asian basketball player.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:41.880
And so it never really felt like I could,

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:44.198
it always felt like I was like

00:18:44.198 --> 00:18:46.110
for, I guess, a good reason

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.100
they respected that I
could play basketball,

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:50.730
but it was always kind of
like, "Oh, he's the Asian."

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.750
And so that was just something
that I kind of got used to,

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:55.770
but that was my experience.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:58.210
And very similar to Dr. Choo,

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.250
just a lot of things
weren't really talked about

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:01.960
when it came to mental health,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.460
even things I had to learn about,

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:05.180
many things I learned about

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.100
once I became a professional
basketball player,

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:08.560
and start just have to tap

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:12.520
into the mind and sports
psychology a little bit more,

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:15.280
but it was something
where even as of late,

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:17.570
when I started to go to therapy

00:19:18.691 --> 00:19:22.120
one of the initial
questions that was asked me

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:24.580
by one of my parents
like, "Oh, what's wrong?

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:27.647
Like, "Are you sure? You
might not need that."

00:19:27.647 --> 00:19:30.740
And it was not ill intentioned at all.

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:35.230
It was just, it's like,
"Oh no, you don't need it,"

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:37.940
or "You need medication?" or "No, no, no."

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:39.930
Like immediately it's
like, 'Oh, what's wrong."

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:42.760
Instead of it kind of being seen as,

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:46.153
this is just a very helpful life tool

00:19:46.153 --> 00:19:49.370
that can be used to process emotions,

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:50.830
to get through certain things,

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:52.960
to be able to just be a better person,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.967
like you would want to eat
healthy, you wanna work out,

00:19:54.967 --> 00:19:56.680
you wanna sleep well,

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:59.870
and you wanna do things for
your holistic wellbeing.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:02.450
This is definitely, very important part,

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:04.093
that plays into that.

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:07.270
- Yeah. Appreciate you sharing that.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:09.486
And again, I mean, there's
so many overlapping themes

00:20:09.486 --> 00:20:12.290
as you were talking between, both of you

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:14.371
just in terms of navigating
different spaces,

00:20:14.371 --> 00:20:17.390
being here in the States,
having those family connections

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:18.550
and sometimes other connections,

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:20.740
but then also kind of trying to navigate

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.160
between different
cultural groups, as well.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:24.640
But I mean, it also seems
like the theme of that

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.280
was just the mental health component

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:28.810
wasn't really talked about all that much.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:30.900
Now I'd say, maybe as an outsider,

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:32.230
that's kind of been similar

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:33.500
to some of my perceptions, as well.

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:34.333
So in college,

00:20:34.333 --> 00:20:38.000
I did have a couple of Korean
American roommates, as well.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.000
I did join them at their
Korean churches at times.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.877
So I got to dabble here and there

00:20:41.877 --> 00:20:44.050
and experience some of the culture.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:45.740
It's funny, I remember
at one point also playing

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:48.210
and I can't remember
the name of the league,

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.860
but there was like a basketball tournament

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.160
at the University of
North Carolina Chapel Hill

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:54.896
for one of the Asian Student Associations.

00:20:54.896 --> 00:20:58.660
Every team was allowed one
non-Asian basketball player.

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:00.860
Of course, every non-Asian
basketball player was black,

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:03.300
so ended up being those two groups.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:04.133
I don't know why I went there,

00:21:04.133 --> 00:21:07.050
but so maybe Jeremy
talking about basketball

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.150
just reminded me of that,

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.060
that's its own interesting phenomenon,

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:13.670
but just kind of some
of those cultural pieces

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.310
that I've just seen them navigate

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:17.790
and have been able to
navigate with them, as well.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.170
But again, just that mental health piece,

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:22.047
I don't ever remember
that being talked about

00:21:22.047 --> 00:21:24.843
and this is late '90s, early 2000s.

00:21:24.843 --> 00:21:27.100
And then even the 2010s,

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:29.170
there were lots of different articles

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:30.080
from different colleges

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.093
where people would talk about,

00:21:31.093 --> 00:21:33.550
the silent struggles that
students were having.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.650
Often they were talking about
Asian-American communities,

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:36.780
black and brown communities,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:38.760
just that people were kind of navigating

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.610
and struggling and walking
through in silence,

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:43.240
not feeling like they had
any space to talk about it,

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:45.620
or they would get some help on campus,

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:47.120
but then go home and feel like

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.460
they couldn't bring that
same conversation up

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:51.550
because of all the different ways

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.410
that things were looked at.

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:55.682
So, I mean, I think I'm mirroring a lot

00:21:55.682 --> 00:21:56.515
of what you all have said,

00:21:56.515 --> 00:21:59.400
and I'm just curious if you've
seen things shift in a sense.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.520
I mean, Jeremy talked
about going to therapy

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.090
after you talked about knowing

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:04.100
what some of those things are now.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:07.185
Have you seen things shift
in terms of acknowledgement

00:22:07.185 --> 00:22:08.780
in different AAPI communities?

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:11.400
Or do you think it's more
at the individual level

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:13.150
at this point or a mixture of both?

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:16.010
- In my personal experience,

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:18.760
I've definitely seen at
least some level of shift

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:23.380
because there's just a lot
more people talking about it,

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:26.720
but maybe it's in certain
circles, or whatever,

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:30.030
but even within my own family,

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.789
as well as a lot of my friends

00:22:32.789 --> 00:22:34.440
as they start to talk about it,

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:38.070
because mental health has
become one of the biggest things

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:39.500
since the pandemic

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:42.140
that at least, certain areas,

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:44.120
are starting to make pushes for

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:45.610
in terms of explosion awareness.

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:50.610
And so even, in my foundation,
just we talked to some kids,

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:52.550
from some of the grantees

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:53.383
and they're talking about,

00:22:53.383 --> 00:22:55.040
and they're going home and
talking to their parents

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:55.873
and they're talking about

00:22:55.873 --> 00:22:58.700
how they need more mental health
resources in their schools.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.610
And it's definitely a different vibe

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:02.121
than when I was growing up

00:23:02.121 --> 00:23:04.693
and I think even after,

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:07.400
between me and some of my friends

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:10.350
and us talking about our
mental health with our parents,

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:13.020
I started to see some
different shifts happening.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:18.000
I mean, a great example that
I had, was this past year

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.370
I went to eat with like
six or seven people,

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:26.370
And they're primarily my agents in China

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:31.108
and as well as another athlete in the CBA,

00:23:31.108 --> 00:23:34.050
another basketball player
in the CBA and some friends,

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:35.334
and as we were all eating,

00:23:35.334 --> 00:23:38.250
we started to kind of
talk about panic attacks.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.250
And so we had mentioned this
concept of panic attacks

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:42.707
and immediately one person was like,

00:23:42.707 --> 00:23:44.422
"Oh is it, really, that happens?.

00:23:44.422 --> 00:23:48.770
Like, I didn't realize
that like, wait so,"

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:50.180
and he started asking these questions

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:52.993
and he was like, "Man,
they can't be that common."

00:23:52.993 --> 00:23:55.560
What ended up happening by
the end of the conversation,

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:56.881
everybody went around

00:23:56.881 --> 00:23:59.540
and said that they have had panic attacks,

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:00.820
multiple panic attacks.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:02.310
And this group of friends,

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:04.700
has been friends for over a decade

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:07.710
and they'd never talked about
it and never knew about it

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:08.620
and never heard of it.

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:10.650
And every single person
went around talking

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.040
about how they had a panic attack here,

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:14.840
or this person struggled
panic attacks occasionally,

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:16.054
or this person had one here.

00:24:16.054 --> 00:24:18.340
And all of a sudden it was like,

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:19.890
at the end of the conversation,

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:23.147
the guy was like, my agent was like,

00:24:23.147 --> 00:24:27.220
"Wow, so it looks like I might
be the only one at this table

00:24:30.097 --> 00:24:32.760
who has no idea what this feels like.

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:37.710
And so it was just
really eye opening to me

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:41.310
to realize, like, because when
you go through these things,

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:42.330
you feel alone

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.410
and you feel like you
don't know who to talk to.

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:46.577
There's no one else that
you can talk to about

00:24:46.577 --> 00:24:48.790
and so that kind of
really challenged me too,

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.168
to like to continue to
try to use my voice,

00:24:51.168 --> 00:24:55.805
my platform more and to kind
of fight more for mental health

00:24:55.805 --> 00:24:57.950
and breaking that stigma.

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:00.070
But I do think to answer
your question there,

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:02.510
we are headed in the
right direction at least.

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:03.560
- Yeah, yeah.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:04.970
I mean, that's a really
helpful story to hear,

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:07.020
I mean, just the way that came about too,

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.430
just the awareness,
the level of awareness.

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:10.263
'Cause like you said,

00:25:10.263 --> 00:25:11.820
everybody was kind of going
through it in isolation

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:13.300
and the one person who had an experience

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.144
had no idea how common it was.

00:25:15.144 --> 00:25:17.443
So yeah, that's really telling.

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:21.620
Esther, have you seen things
shift from your perspective?

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.220
- Yeah, I'm sort of going in circles

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:25.680
as I listen to Jeremy's story

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:30.270
because I do think we're
still so early in this process

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:35.270
as an Asian-American community,
as a global Asian community.

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:38.203
I mean, I think of how,

00:25:40.299 --> 00:25:42.740
I mean, I almost remember to the day,

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:44.300
or at least the week or the era,

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.760
when I had conversations with my friends

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.340
about David Chang, the chef,

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.240
talking about his mental health

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:54.370
and how that was such a big
deal when he was so open

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:55.660
and wrote about it in his book.

00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:57.180
And I remember mentioning it to a friend,

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.650
and Jeremy, your name came up

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:03.460
because it's like, it's
really cool to see,

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:05.330
even athletes, Asian athletes

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:07.350
talking about mental health like that.

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:09.290
And it's still in this era,

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:13.009
where when somebody who's
recognized and like comfortable

00:26:13.009 --> 00:26:14.700
that you relate to,

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:17.890
like you guys talking about this topic,

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:18.890
it's still one of those,

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:21.250
like, people are surprised,

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:22.840
it's like this in a wonderful way,

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:25.490
but I still hear the
surprise in people's voices.

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:27.865
It's not like, oh yeah,

00:26:27.865 --> 00:26:29.690
the 500th famous Asian person we know

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:31.790
is talking about mental
health, they all talk.

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:33.670
It's still this wow moment

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:35.060
because you don't,

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.400
I still react with surprise

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:38.270
and I mean, I'm much older than you are.

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:41.040
So I am carrying with me

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:43.180
kind of the expectations and norms

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:45.380
of a slightly different generation.

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:48.098
But I think for me and
my peers, there's this,

00:26:48.098 --> 00:26:50.670
oh yeah, we can talk about this now.

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:52.910
It's like, we've all been
quietly going to therapy,

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:54.410
but now we can talk about this.

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:56.353
But I do see it more.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:00.650
I do feel like the response is so positive

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:04.623
now that people are really
loving to see those messages.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:07.917
I think from a researcher's perspective,

00:27:07.917 --> 00:27:10.400
'cause so much of my time is in research.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.841
When we talk about Asian
American and Pacific Islander

00:27:13.841 --> 00:27:16.760
health disparities around mental health,

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:19.900
so much of it is just
the numbers part of it,

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:22.710
because of the reluctance to report

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.420
kind of collides with
our poor data collection

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:29.170
when it comes to any health problems

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:31.760
among Asian-Americans
and Pacific Islanders.

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:33.890
So I think, when you
think about all the steps

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:36.150
you need to take to appropriately
address a health problem,

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.840
you have to like understand the problem

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:38.770
and collect the data

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.120
and then devote the
appropriate resources to it

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.610
and develop good target outcomes

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:46.380
and you go through that
whole process a lot of times.

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:49.870
With Asian American
mental health problems,

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.970
we're really starting
at the data collection,

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:53.070
understanding the problem

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:58.070
because it's been so hard
to get people to report,

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:04.353
to seek help, to register as as a dot,

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:09.470
when you're looking at
all the health services

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:10.490
that are used, for example.

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:13.470
So I think some days

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:15.460
I feel like, wow, we've got so far to go

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:16.293
to actually feel like

00:28:16.293 --> 00:28:18.940
we're robustly addressing
mental health problems.

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:20.730
And then there's times where I feel like

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:23.680
there's so many, increasingly
more inspirational stories

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.360
and people so hungry to hear those stories

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:28.395
and feel like, okay, like
that conversation can happen.

00:28:28.395 --> 00:28:31.760
And people like Jeremy and David,

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:33.630
are making these huge,

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:36.450
it's like (imitating the
sound of an explosion)

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:37.900
just like a single voice like that.

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:39.810
I feel like has these huge ripple effects,

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:41.970
it gives people permission
to have those conversations

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.470
with their friends,

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:46.187
to use people that are well known

00:28:46.187 --> 00:28:48.660
as kind of a starting point
for those conversations.

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.350
And that didn't happen when I grew up.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:54.780
Never, never, I can't think
of a single Asian person

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.590
that I looked up to who
really used mental health

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:00.210
as like a major part of what
they wanted to talk about.

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:03.310
So just hearing it, seeing it now

00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.010
is incredibly moving
and makes me feel like,

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:07.270
ah, there's hope.

00:29:07.270 --> 00:29:08.460
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.030
I mean, it sounds like
a breath of fresh air

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:11.680
in a lot of ways too,

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:13.410
which is really, really important.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:15.800
I mean, you also highlight
so many important topics

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:17.670
of where things need to move also,

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:22.350
in terms of some of the one
to two year old statistics

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:25.760
saying that 23% of those
from the AAPI community

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.360
actually seek out help.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.270
So, Jeremy, even as you
were sharing your story,

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:30.440
part of what I was wondering

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:32.300
was like, okay, well, all
those people were hearing that

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:34.400
from each other for the first time,

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:36.110
one layer of me wonders like,

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:38.680
did people actually seek out help

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:40.110
when they were going
through those panic attacks

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:43.760
or were people walking
through that in isolation?

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.500
And so like I had a mixed feeling

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:47.520
like I was, oh, I'm so glad
this is actually happening

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:50.000
in terms of the conversations,
people are realizing,

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.539
but I'm also hoping that that level

00:29:52.539 --> 00:29:54.510
of not having gotten to that point,

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:57.607
isn't happening on a larger
scale in some ways to,

00:29:57.607 --> 00:29:58.900
not to be pessimistic,

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:00.690
but that's kind of where my mind went

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:01.540
hearing both of those.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:03.820
And so I wonder if you
all would be willing

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:05.610
to talk about that aspect too.

00:30:05.610 --> 00:30:09.530
Like, are there ways that we
as a society or individuals

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:11.550
can help people move to that place

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:12.880
of actually wanting to seek out their help

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:14.150
or just still the stigma

00:30:14.150 --> 00:30:15.900
that really needs to kind
of be broken down first?

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.230
Like where do you see
some of the tension points

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:20.143
and areas where we can move that forward?

00:30:22.503 --> 00:30:26.210
- Okay, I'll go, I guess.
- I'm leaving it to you,

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:27.043
open ended.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.660
- I agree a lot with Dr. Choo,

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:36.160
that a lot of times when
you say these things,

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.873
there's a lot of shock.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:42.290
But then by the end of the conversation,

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.240
it seems to always moving a good place.

00:30:45.444 --> 00:30:47.143
So going back to that story,

00:30:48.746 --> 00:30:51.010
I shared, I was going through therapy.

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:54.260
They were there with me
when, not in the room,

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:56.940
but they were waiting for me to get out,

00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:59.140
when I went through that 11 week period

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:01.580
of being confined in a very small room.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:04.040
And that's when I started therapy

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.873
and so as I was talking
about it with them,

00:31:08.902 --> 00:31:11.460
there's mixed reactions

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:12.480
for some people when they were talking

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:13.420
about their panic attacks,

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:14.870
like some people they didn't even know,

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:17.945
they're like, "Oh, so it's
called a panic attack."

00:31:17.945 --> 00:31:18.990
Like people were like, "Oh."

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.030
Another friend was like,
I actually went online

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:21.987
and I researched and I was like,

00:31:21.987 --> 00:31:24.900
"What is this and why is this happening?"

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:28.510
And then that's how he came to
realize it was panic attacks.

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:33.510
Then other people have figured it out

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:36.800
and trying to do something about it.

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:39.520
And so this is an array

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:42.050
of kind of how everybody deals with it,

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:44.320
but the truth is the reality is still

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:46.310
that like nobody at that table

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.040
ever talked about with
anybody else at that table

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:51.592
and didn't know that
everyone was going through it

00:31:51.592 --> 00:31:55.480
and so for me, it's like, again,

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:59.920
it's like you're scratching the surface

00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:02.003
of what it could or should be.

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:05.687
But at least now we're
scratching the surface

00:32:05.687 --> 00:32:08.963
and we're kind of starting
to bang at that door.

00:32:10.911 --> 00:32:13.380
And even for me, when
I share to tell people

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:14.940
like, "Hey, I started therapy."

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:17.950
I almost have to like, my eyes are open,

00:32:17.950 --> 00:32:20.760
but in my mind I almost close
my eyes for next three seconds

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:22.450
because every time I say that

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.187
a lot of people are like, are like,

00:32:24.187 --> 00:32:26.384
"Wait, what happened?,"
or are like, "Why?"

00:32:26.384 --> 00:32:30.290
And so I have to like blind
myself to like the shock.

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:32.450
Otherwise it'll make me
feel a little insecure,

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:34.040
like, "Ooh, shoot somethings wrong with me

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:36.510
or what am I doing or
am I sure I need it."

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:39.027
And so even when I say it,
I kind of just say like,

00:32:39.027 --> 00:32:41.000
"Hey, let them have the
reaction they're gonna have,"

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:42.160
and then explain it

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.360
and talk about it a
little bit more in depth.

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:46.150
And typically by the end,

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:47.260
and it's kind of what you said

00:32:47.260 --> 00:32:48.370
on the beginning of this podcast,

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:50.300
like authenticity always works the best.

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:51.624
And if I just share for my heart,

00:32:51.624 --> 00:32:54.460
they kind of realize, "Oh, wow,

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:57.740
I probably struggle with
some level of anxiety.

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:01.040
I probably have some of
these foreboding thoughts.

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:04.490
I probably process emotions inadequately,

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.230
and experiences inadequately

00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:08.297
that affect me in the long term."

00:33:08.297 --> 00:33:11.160
And so I think there's
just a certain level

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:13.560
of, again, you're breaking
through that stigma

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:16.160
of like, "Oh, just because I go to therapy

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:17.770
means X, Y, or Z

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:19.720
and that's not where I'm
at, so I don't need that.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:21.170
I'm not like one of them,"

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:24.800
is kind of the mentality
that some people might have.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:28.000
So, yeah, I agree,
there's a long way to go,

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:29.018
but at least we're starting

00:33:29.018 --> 00:33:32.980
and it does seem like for the most part,

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:35.030
I've had much more stories

00:33:35.030 --> 00:33:38.370
of people being very
positive and receptive

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.525
about hearing about it than the other way.

00:33:41.525 --> 00:33:42.760
- I think that's really great.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:44.530
You're giving people that grace too.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:45.950
I mean that that's a fine line to walk,

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:47.670
giving them the grace to kind of react,

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:50.167
but then also protecting
yourself in a sense too,

00:33:50.167 --> 00:33:52.230
so you don't start to second guess

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:54.700
the choices and decisions
that you've made,

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:56.230
which are also very, very helpful.

00:33:56.230 --> 00:33:59.429
So definitely commend that
'cause that's not easy.

00:33:59.429 --> 00:34:01.290
- Can I ask, I'm sorry,

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:03.010
I'm so sorry if this is off topic,

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.323
I'm really curious about this 11 weeks.

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:09.663
Did you know going in it would
be about that time period?

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:14.740
Was it persistent
positive tests or symptoms

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:16.310
or how did it end up being,

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:17.960
that seems like a very long time.

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:23.785
- Yeah, well, I think, generally speaking,

00:34:23.785 --> 00:34:27.340
the test standards here

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:32.240
is about, I would say, like
three to 4x more strict

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:33.860
in terms of like the bacteria count.

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:38.860
And so the typical, I remember, for me,

00:34:39.377 --> 00:34:41.803
it was like they were expecting,

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:45.700
on average two to three
weeks and I'd be fine.

00:34:45.700 --> 00:34:50.700
The problem is I have a broken nose

00:34:50.810 --> 00:34:53.150
I broke my nose many
times from basketball.

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:57.210
and so I couldn't flush out the dead virus

00:34:57.210 --> 00:34:59.258
from my nasal cavity fast enough.

00:34:59.258 --> 00:35:04.240
And so I would have these like
really inconsistent tests.

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:07.760
And so I would have a
ton of dead bacteria,

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:08.593
but it was getting picked up

00:35:08.593 --> 00:35:11.430
and it was enough to be positive.

00:35:11.430 --> 00:35:12.780
So what it was actually pretty crazy

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:15.660
is there's a protocol for how you test out

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:17.800
and you had to get negative
on back to back tests.

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:22.670
And so I did that and I
had the hospital COVID test

00:35:22.670 --> 00:35:25.410
and then I had the CDC
COVID test, now it's fine.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:29.300
And I went back into to restart
my 21 day hotel quarantine.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:33.520
And then on the seventh
day, I retested positive,

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:36.033
even though for me, I was vaccinated.

00:35:36.033 --> 00:35:40.179
And so there was a lot of yeah.

00:35:40.179 --> 00:35:44.500
or, sorry, not bacteria,
virus, but the dead virus.

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:45.920
And so that was the main issue.

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:49.230
And so I had to go back
again and then stay there

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:54.185
for a few more weeks until it
came back completely clean.

00:35:54.185 --> 00:35:58.430
It was tough because like this whole time,

00:35:58.430 --> 00:36:01.320
my whole like signature
shoe launch, my Asia tour,

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:02.270
the start of my season,

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:06.059
like everything that I had
prepared for, I lost all of it.

00:36:06.059 --> 00:36:09.141
And so I missed a third of my season

00:36:09.141 --> 00:36:11.930
and because I was vaccinated,

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:14.433
I actually was asymptomatic for,

00:36:15.992 --> 00:36:17.670
out of those 77 days,

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.960
I was asymptomatic for the
last, like 60 something.

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:24.220
And I never had too many symptoms,

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:26.370
but that's just how it goes.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:30.610
And so really to be honest, at that point,

00:36:30.610 --> 00:36:32.710
it was more about like my body.

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:36.957
I think I lost like 15,
20 pounds of like weight

00:36:36.957 --> 00:36:40.430
because I couldn't work out

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:43.120
and I couldn't get
enough food in my system.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:45.770
And then also just my mind,

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:48.950
and that's where I'll be very transparent

00:36:48.950 --> 00:36:52.380
is like being in this
tiny little hospital room

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:55.970
and there's no windows
that directly see outside.

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.000
And by the end it was like,

00:36:58.000 --> 00:36:59.880
I didn't even want to be awake.

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:04.880
And so I started to have this
suicidal ideation of like man,

00:37:07.050 --> 00:37:08.330
and my goal every day

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:10.396
was to try to wake up as late as possible

00:37:10.396 --> 00:37:13.840
and to try to be as awake
for as little as possible.

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:14.673
There were days

00:37:14.673 --> 00:37:17.110
where I wouldn't even
want to get out of bed.

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:18.181
I would just be laying there

00:37:18.181 --> 00:37:20.340
and breakfast would be put there.

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:21.740
The nurse would walk in, put breakfast

00:37:21.740 --> 00:37:23.060
and the nurse would walk in, put lunch,

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:26.840
and I wouldn't even touch the
food or turn on the lights,

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:27.810
and they're like, "Are you okay?

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:28.840
Are you still sleeping?"

00:37:28.840 --> 00:37:31.440
And it's just like, I have no motivation.

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:33.108
I can't stare at these four walls anymore.

00:37:33.108 --> 00:37:36.687
Like talking to myself all day.

00:37:36.687 --> 00:37:39.680
And so that was when I
really hit a low point.

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:41.850
And I was like, yeah,
I'm gonna go to therapy.

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:44.090
And I started therapy

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:46.880
and in the past, I've worked
with sports psychologists

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:48.180
and other things like that

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:52.980
but this was like for me
as a person holistically.

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:56.370
and so that was my experience.

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:58.460
It was something that
- Oh my gosh

00:37:58.460 --> 00:38:00.313
- I'll never forget for sure.

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:06.790
- Especially that piece where
you moved towards getting out

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:08.550
and then got yanked back in

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:11.990
just must have been so
crushing to have that hope

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:14.913
and then to be back to square one.

00:38:16.822 --> 00:38:21.480
I think a lot of it is about expectations.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:23.070
If you're told from the outset

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:24.980
that it's gonna be this amount of time,

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:28.300
but to have it kind of so
uncertain the entire time

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:33.300
must have just been crushing
for your brain and your spirit.

00:38:33.610 --> 00:38:35.750
- Oh, it was so crushing

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:37.920
because the thing is like,

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.170
my first negative test came on day 10,

00:38:41.170 --> 00:38:42.810
but I didn't officially clear out

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.830
of the entire thing until day 77.

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:47.510
So for the last 67 days,

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:50.910
I'm thinking I'm almost
there, I'm almost there.

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:53.950
And again, it's called (speaking
in a foreign language),

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:56.060
when you retest positive,

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:59.743
I was so upset that like
my luggage and things,

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:02.210
there were some very expensive

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:05.060
some stuff where I was like
I don't even want it anymore.

00:39:06.381 --> 00:39:09.380
I was so angry and I was sweating

00:39:09.380 --> 00:39:10.760
and like, no, this can't be happening.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:12.080
It was like a nightmare.

00:39:12.080 --> 00:39:15.080
And then like, you take the
test at 5:30 in the morning,

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:18.470
you need to wait until 9:00 PM
at night to get your results.

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:21.240
And so like the whole day is like,

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:23.360
I'm just like, and every
time a nurse walks by,

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:24.760
I'm like, "Oh, a nurse just walked by.

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:26.850
Are they coming in? Oh,
it's bad news again."

00:39:26.850 --> 00:39:29.980
And it's just like thing

00:39:29.980 --> 00:39:31.420
where on the outside looking in,

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:34.990
if I tell the story now I'm
like, oh, it sounds bad.

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:36.940
But to actually go through it

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:39.400
and to go through that
for 11 straight weeks

00:39:39.400 --> 00:39:42.020
watching all my hard work and everything

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.060
that I prepared for dwindle

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:46.780
and watching my body just atrophy.

00:39:46.780 --> 00:39:50.890
It was like, and like you
said, the expectation of it.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:52.470
The up and down, that was a killer.

00:39:52.470 --> 00:39:54.830
If I came in and someone's just
like, it's gonna be 77 days,

00:39:54.830 --> 00:39:56.651
I'll be like, all right,
mentally lock in, I'm good.

00:39:56.651 --> 00:39:58.500
I just gotta find a
way to make it through.

00:39:58.500 --> 00:39:59.650
I got a little countdown,

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:00.994
but it's because I kept thinking

00:40:00.994 --> 00:40:03.240
I'm right around the corner,
I'm right around the corner.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:04.853
And then it'll be like, just kidding.

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.060
- That's wild.
- That's tough.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:11.293
- It does make me think too about,

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:14.130
it's something you said really early on,

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:16.260
which is that mental
health is just health.

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.810
And I mean, when you described
I going through that,

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:22.340
I actually think it would be abnormal

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:25.300
if you didn't have an adverse
mental health consequence

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:26.133
from that,

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:30.100
just kind of like you're
in a big car accident

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:32.500
and it's normal for you to be injured.

00:40:32.500 --> 00:40:35.893
It's like, I don't actually
know any person on earth,

00:40:36.790 --> 00:40:38.320
no matter how mentally
strong they think are,

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:41.210
that could go through that
experience and not have,

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.640
just like we talked a lot
about that in healthcare,

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:45.640
how in the pandemic,

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:48.010
when we were all like in
the hospital all the time,

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:50.110
we were fearing for
ourselves and our families

00:40:50.110 --> 00:40:52.505
and watching these people
drown in their own secretions.

00:40:52.505 --> 00:40:55.470
And there was so much
uncertainty and we were scared

00:40:55.470 --> 00:40:57.377
and people are like, yeah,
you know, if you need it,

00:40:57.377 --> 00:40:59.290
we have these resources for mental health.

00:40:59.290 --> 00:41:01.810
And at some point we are kind of like,

00:41:01.810 --> 00:41:02.643
what if we just say,

00:41:02.643 --> 00:41:04.560
we're gonna assume you
all need mental health

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:06.670
because in this circumstance,

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:09.970
having an adverse symptom
of mental health is normal,

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:13.610
like who would not have
anxiety, depression,

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:15.101
hopelessness, PTSD.

00:41:15.101 --> 00:41:18.610
So let's just package in
some mental health there

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:20.740
and if you don't need
that, you can opt out.

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:24.423
I just feel like, could
we have a more proactive,

00:41:25.540 --> 00:41:29.160
especially for people who
have a hard time asking for it

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:32.410
because of cultural reasons and norms,

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:35.180
it seems like maybe the
system can do a little more,

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:37.270
maybe healthcare can do a little more

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:39.100
to say a normal symptom

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:42.710
would be for you to need
some therapy, medication,

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:45.580
support groups around now, you know?

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:47.990
But I mean that your experience is,

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:51.210
I mean, that's gotta be a
very extreme human experience

00:41:51.210 --> 00:41:53.263
to go through and I cannot imagine.

00:41:55.036 --> 00:41:59.636
- Yeah. I mean, everything
you said is so, so important.

00:41:59.636 --> 00:42:01.560
Yeah, 'cause Jeremy, as
you're describing that,

00:42:01.560 --> 00:42:04.230
I mean all the (indistinct),
like what we do in studies,

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:06.187
basically you went through all that.

00:42:06.187 --> 00:42:08.910
If you just add on
uncertainty over uncertainty,

00:42:08.910 --> 00:42:11.970
over uncertainty, changing
expectations, lack of motivation,

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:15.170
like those are all the things
like from a brain standpoint

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:19.010
that lead people to just that
state of being overwhelmed,

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:21.390
not wanting to get up,
get outta bed, do things.

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:23.070
So, I mean, exactly as you were saying

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:26.660
like just needing to have those
things in place, proactively

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:27.875
I think is so important

00:42:27.875 --> 00:42:29.290
because in a lot of ways,

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:30.311
when we're trying to look at those things

00:42:30.311 --> 00:42:32.810
in laboratory settings,

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:35.350
we're trying to model
chronic unpredictable stress

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:36.577
is the term that people use.

00:42:36.577 --> 00:42:38.870
And that's exactly like, as you described,

00:42:38.870 --> 00:42:40.387
that's exactly what
you were going through.

00:42:40.387 --> 00:42:42.425
And I think what made it so difficult?

00:42:42.425 --> 00:42:45.840
But also, I'm encouraged
that you took the time

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:49.150
to say, okay, I am actually
going to look into therapy

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:51.410
and not just let this be the way it is

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.462
and to break through that, as well.

00:42:53.462 --> 00:42:55.550
I mean, one question I also wanted ask you

00:42:55.550 --> 00:42:57.780
and have Esther also chime in,

00:42:57.780 --> 00:42:59.410
because I can only
imagine there's so much,

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:01.460
you must have learned going through that

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:02.654
because it was so difficult.

00:43:02.654 --> 00:43:04.930
What are the things that
you've taken from that,

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:08.350
that you're trying to
hold onto moving forward?

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:10.700
Because I would imagine there are lessons

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:12.040
and things that you've
learned in that moment

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:14.740
that you don't just want to
keep there in that moment.

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:20.050
- Yeah, I think tying it back
into all of mental health,

00:43:20.050 --> 00:43:25.050
I think one of the lessons that I learned

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:28.990
is when people decide

00:43:28.990 --> 00:43:31.300
whether they need mental health support,

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:34.350
they're acting as their own doctor.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:38.090
And so if you ask somebody
who smokes regularly,

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:39.770
they'll be like, "Oh, I'm okay.

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:41.660
Like I can still play
basketball, I can still work out.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:42.690
I can still do,"

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:45.920
and it's like, "Yeah, but
if you go on long enough,

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:47.760
like there's gonna be adverse effects,"

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:49.150
but they're their own doctor.

00:43:49.150 --> 00:43:51.830
And what I realized with my mental health

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:52.830
was even in the past,

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:54.410
when I had been more resistant to it,

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:56.010
it was like, I don't

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:57.950
and it's what Dr. Choo just said,

00:43:57.950 --> 00:43:59.983
no one comes out the same.

00:44:01.287 --> 00:44:05.020
That's a fact but it's
whether you realize it or not.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:08.126
And so for me, I realized
a lot of these experiences

00:44:08.126 --> 00:44:10.390
were having effects,

00:44:10.390 --> 00:44:12.400
I just didn't realize it at the time.

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:15.910
And so compartmentalizing
it or sweeping under the rug

00:44:15.910 --> 00:44:19.890
does not count as like I'm okay,

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:21.170
because that will build up

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:22.580
and that will come back to get you

00:44:22.580 --> 00:44:25.780
somewhere later down the
road in a different way.

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:27.430
And you have to be aware of that.

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:29.780
And so that's my like spiel

00:44:29.780 --> 00:44:32.520
on saying that when I went
through this situation,

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:35.090
when I got out, I was
like, I'm finally out.

00:44:35.090 --> 00:44:37.090
and I had this like breath of fresh air,

00:44:37.090 --> 00:44:38.630
even just to feel the sun,

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:40.640
even to be able to walk down the hall,

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.750
walk out that door and walk down the hall

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:44.490
or to go down an elevator.

00:44:44.490 --> 00:44:45.593
Like these things.

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:48.420
My first time walking down the street,

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:49.590
I almost was freaking out

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:51.570
I saw all the lights, the cars,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:53.442
because I was like there's so much noise.

00:44:53.442 --> 00:44:57.330
And I was just so excited to live life

00:44:57.330 --> 00:45:00.050
and I was like, okay, that part is over.

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:01.650
And then I got into the season

00:45:01.650 --> 00:45:03.780
and in the season I
started off really poorly

00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:07.045
because my body was
inactive for three months.

00:45:07.045 --> 00:45:09.360
And I realized during the season,

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.670
I remember after one of my worst games,

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:14.780
I was like, I'm gonna quit,
I just want to go home.

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:17.183
Like, I don't wanna
play basketball anymore.

00:45:20.130 --> 00:45:23.700
After a bad game, I wouldn't
normally respond that way.

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:27.120
And I started to think like,
why am I so lacking motivation?

00:45:27.120 --> 00:45:30.760
Why do I want out so bad?
What is wrong with my mindset?

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:33.200
Like, I've never been like
this in my whole career.

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:34.630
And that's when I realized,

00:45:34.630 --> 00:45:35.557
okay, what happened there

00:45:35.557 --> 00:45:38.140
and the amount of energy and the damage

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:41.960
and the trauma that I
took, is affecting me now,

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:45.100
even though I didn't expect it to happen.

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:47.010
And so I think that's really important

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:50.040
to know that as you go
through these experiences,

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:51.840
they're building up and
they're changing you

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:53.180
and are changing the way you think,

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:54.770
and they're changing like for me,

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:57.160
they changed my persistence
factor and my perseverance,

00:45:57.160 --> 00:45:59.860
because I just went
through something that.

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:01.530
And so again, if you ask
me, when I first got out,

00:46:01.530 --> 00:46:03.700
I'd be like, dude, I'm ready,
I'm ready for the season.

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:05.780
I'm ready for basketball,
like, I'm tired of every,

00:46:05.780 --> 00:46:07.230
I'm so excited.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:10.800
Now, the minute that at basketball
started, I realize, dang,

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:13.500
I have almost have nothing,
I'm running on empty tank.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:19.050
And so that's like one of
the biggest things I learned

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:21.410
is that like, if you're not processing

00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:24.720
and healthily processing
everything you're going through,

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:27.630
it is continuing to
change your subconscious

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:30.950
and the way that you think
over and over and over again.

00:46:30.950 --> 00:46:33.780
And that, like I said,
you don't wanna wait

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:37.510
until the person who's
smoking two packs a day

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:41.030
does not want to wait
until the worst diagnosis

00:46:41.030 --> 00:46:43.010
to go and do something about it, right?

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:46.960
And so that would be maybe
an analogy I would use yeah,

00:46:46.960 --> 00:46:49.300
for what I experienced
with my mental health.

00:46:49.300 --> 00:46:50.390
- Yeah, that's great.

00:46:50.390 --> 00:46:52.757
That's really, really
telling in a lot of ways too.

00:46:52.757 --> 00:46:54.510
I'm not gonna jump been
to all neuroscience,

00:46:54.510 --> 00:46:56.230
even though I have that tendency,

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:58.880
but just to mention like
everything you said,

00:46:58.880 --> 00:46:59.827
like we often talk about,

00:46:59.827 --> 00:47:02.210
the experiences they change our brains,

00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:03.456
whether that's positive experiences

00:47:03.456 --> 00:47:06.240
or challenging traumatic experiences,

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.020
like what you had gone
through and that has effects.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:09.580
And so you were seeing some of that happen

00:47:09.580 --> 00:47:11.500
even though you weren't
in that situation anymore.

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:13.090
So I think it's so important

